former NASA astronaut Peggy Whitson  first dreamed of going to space when she  saw Neil Armstrong take that first step  on the moon  Peggy spent a record six hundred and  sixty-five days in space more than any  other American in history she is with  Tony who's at the Kennedy Space Center  for the 50th anniversary of that Apollo  11 launch Tony hey guys she says she  doesn't make it back that much these  days but she remembers being a  nine-year-old in Ojai in Iowa on a farm  when Apollo launched take us back to  that moment  well actually the most memorable part  for me was the the touchdown and the the  first step on the moon I remember that  distinctly it was late at night for me I  was supposed to be in bed and my parents  out of bed so that we could watch it so  I think part of the reason I remember it  is because we got to stay up late to  watch you were inspired by Sally Ride  the first woman in space now there are a  lot of boys and girls watching right now  looking at you and feeling inspired  what's your best advice you've received  for them well the best advice is find  your passion work really really hard and  push yourself challenge yourself to live  outside what's comfortable for you  because that's when you're gonna find  out how much more you can really do  speaking of what's comfortable in a CBS  poll most Americans said they supported  a return to the moon but most Americans  thought the money that it would cost to  get there might be better spent  elsewhere so what's the argument for  going back well I think exploration  pushing the limits pushing our  boundaries is the reason to go back it  improves our technology here on earth  all the money that gets spent on that  space exploration is spent down here on  the ground and we're developing new  technologies so I think it's really  important and Peggy I know you happen to  believe there's more life out there  other than us do you think we'll find it  real quickly I hope so I don't maybe not  on the moon but we'll find it you're  looking for an answer on that as well  you know as we close out today I just  want to say I didn't think it was  possible to get chills in 90-degree heat  but but standing here with Peggy on this  historic day on the very spot where  Cronkite made the call 50 years ago  turns out it is possible  I feel it too because Peggy Whitson is  such a badass so lucky he's got to talk  to her
 

